{"title":"1990 AIME Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Generalization\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Generalization</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution!!!\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution!!!</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_AIME_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Someone observed that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/4/e6491f393308a051ebf0e9605762f36361e6a2d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6! = 8 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"100\" height=\"13\" />.  Find the largest <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Positive\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Positive\">positive</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integer</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/c/a2cd1c2adc0a5a8775c1a15378c3a8ef825966ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^{}_{}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/7/857b4f28463608c489140b6b8395e66b00956156.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^{}_{}!$\" width=\"15\" height=\"13\" /> can be expressed as the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Product\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Product\">product</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d5c7ef46f13dd19edec6af6d3b929fefbb5935.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n - 3_{}^{}$\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Consecutive\" title=\"Consecutive\">consecutive</a> positive integers.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_AIME_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The product of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/1/d41c4438c8dfad08aa1f927b5852d79dc1ea5195.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n - 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> consecutive integers can be written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bb1af6ede22560eb360a1f59e53adc8c0b6f540.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(n - 3 + a)!}{a!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"95\" height=\"38\" /> for some integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/3/6639bbbb69351cb7030990b11661335cf132491d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n! = \\frac{(n - 3 + a)!}{a!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"135\" height=\"38\" />, from which it  becomes evident that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/1/761f953f34c7f838cd0742690928b86e9710a5ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\ge 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/3/e736c5d401b4a8fa1399f5ad17817fcbdfc4c1f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n - 3 + a)! &gt; n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"132\" height=\"18\" />, we can rewrite this as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/4/2b446482b57cd2c155c1f3d0c032d3a000063fa9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n!(n+1)(n+2) \\ldots (n-3+a)}{a!} = n! \\Longrightarrow (n+1)(n+2) \\ldots (n-3+a) = a!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"593\" height=\"38\" />. For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f98b3f405113241659d8c8db2ea3480346db4d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/c/96c1c1c73b53eceb00fee25a46d9076b0383d202.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n + 1 = 4!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"79\" height=\"14\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/7/087272e81dcb5b0c76bb0aa6b4cd9c24a6cdf749.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 23$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" />. For greater values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, we need to find the product of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c36ddba89eb5035af6d0d797c475171b9df6c9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a-3$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> consecutive integers that equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/8/dc80ce189c35cf4877ca2f60fa66815ab167a6a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a!$\" width=\"13\" height=\"13\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> can be approximated as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/3/cf32c7b34a19c8b9f6b37d7f15cba4c09f1cc9d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$^{a-3}\\sqrt{a!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" />, which is a monotonically decreasing function. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/4/b144cd13aa32e12aa70c15886a3721d78f25e54e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = \\boxed{023}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"76\" height=\"25\" /> is the greatest possible value to satisfy the given conditions.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_AIME_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the largest of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/5/31503c8c86ff405ea9ae2dd6285eecb04550cb97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> consecutive positive integers be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. Clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> cannot be less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, else the product of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/5/31503c8c86ff405ea9ae2dd6285eecb04550cb97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> consecutive positive integers will be less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Key observation:\nNow for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> to be maximum the smallest number (or starting number) of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/5/31503c8c86ff405ea9ae2dd6285eecb04550cb97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> consecutive positive integers must be minimum, implying that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> needs to be minimum. But the least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/30236402777f499778852128a0242a5ee88f0f7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k &gt; n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>So the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/5/31503c8c86ff405ea9ae2dd6285eecb04550cb97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> consecutive positive integers are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/f/5ff5deda16c9e2c10a35b004b87f11ee15406dca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5, 6, 7…, n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"116\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>So we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/f/f8fd9b71b980971efe3f749a2b8626df6d645f03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(n+1)!}{4!} = n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"104\" height=\"39\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/2/ae2dcd2d7365a3c2442caa303db4b77c02da1b6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Longrightarrow  n+1 = 24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"121\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/9/4c9a17dc4baebc23ef201d6b3ff317ff8984e26e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Longrightarrow  n = \\boxed{023}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"110\" height=\"25\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Generalization\">Generalization</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_AIME_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Generalization\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Largest positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> can be expressed as the product of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/5/945279d696f597e369523935db4af097c0da09af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" /> consecutive positive integers is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/7/6e7d2fa534cb9d2f348cb0a0d845861ea77baa77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a+1)! - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>For ex. largest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that product of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/1/e8157f07bd655cac2420b656ca7e09e49ff6c26a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-6$\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> consecutive positive integers is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/b/04ba960a63fcf0cf0cdb0a9fba1b9e69809a83de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7!-1 = 5039$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"105\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Proof:\nReasoning the same way as above, let the largest of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/5/945279d696f597e369523935db4af097c0da09af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" /> consecutive positive integers be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. Clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> cannot be less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, else the product of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/5/945279d696f597e369523935db4af097c0da09af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" /> consecutive positive integers will be less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Now, observe that for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> to be maximum the smallest number (or starting number) of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/5/945279d696f597e369523935db4af097c0da09af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" /> consecutive positive integers must be minimum, implying that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> needs to be minimum. But the least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/30236402777f499778852128a0242a5ee88f0f7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k &gt; n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>So the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/5/945279d696f597e369523935db4af097c0da09af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" /> consecutive positive integers are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/1/96150a4d571592da3d1ddf9d6240745977b6380b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+2, a+3, … n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"162\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>So we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6589b336af69205c3e937451beaeb38ada7186.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(n+1)!}{(a+1)!} = n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"104\" height=\"43\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/3/ec3717bebd3faa0f7a23487138dc98bc3bed72f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Longrightarrow  n+1 = (a+1)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"159\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/6/2b679e600f44a4cc885b5ff668e687702e7ec120.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Longrightarrow  n = (a+1)! -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"160\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/8/ab8a050ca0f84a6aef15ead346438c2777533f30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Kris17$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution.21.21.21\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution!!!\">Video Solution!!!</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_AIME_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution!!!\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H-ZmsYjF-xE\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H-ZmsYjF-xE</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_AIME_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_AIME\" title=\"1990 AIME\">1990 AIME </a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_AIME_Problems\" title=\"1990 AIME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_AIME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1990 AIME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=1990\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_AIME_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"1